      "problem": "Express $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3})\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as an inequality.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249809/assets/73f3c747217e98926510918c395eb644eb143ba07446a863b3f038aafb3fd19a.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3})$ can be expressed as the inequality $x<\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $(3,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x>3.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx<\\sqrt{3}\\text{ or }x>3.\n$$",
